  <div class=3D"section highlight">
    <h2><span class=3D"icon">=F0=9F=93=8C</span> LLMs are Stateless</h2>
    <p>
      By default, LLMs are completely stateless. Every call is independent =
- the model has no memory of
      prior conversations, no awareness of who the user is, and no knowledg=
e of what it did in a previous session.
      Each request starts from zero. Memory is the layer engineers add on t=
op to give agents continuity,
      personalization, and the ability to reason across time.
    </p>

    <div class=3D"note-box" style=3D"margin: 0.75rem 0">
      <strong>Two fundamental layers:</strong> <em>Short-term memory</em> h=
andles the current task or session.
      <em>Long-term memory</em> persists facts, events, and skills across t=
asks and sessions.
      Production agentic systems need both.
    </div>

    <div class=3D"grid-2" style=3D"margin-top:1rem">
      <div class=3D"card blue">
        <h3>Short-Term Memory</h3>
        <p>Everything relevant to the <strong>current task or session</stro=
ng> - conversation history, current plan, tool outputs, intermediate reason=
ing, agent state.</p>
        <span class=3D"tag blue">Session-scoped</span>
      </div>
      <div class=3D"card green">
        <h3>Long-Term Memory</h3>
        <p>Information retained <strong>across tasks and sessions</strong> =
- user facts, past events, skills, and workflows. Survives indefinitely.</p=
>
        <span class=3D"tag green">Cross-session</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

