  <div class=3D"section highlight">
    <h2><span class=3D"icon">=F0=9F=97=84</span> Long-Term Memory</h2>
    <p>
      Long-term memory persists <strong>outside the context window</strong>=
 in external stores.
      It survives across sessions, and is selectively retrieved into
      context when relevant.
    </p>

    <div class=3D"grid-3" style=3D"margin-top:1rem">
      <div class=3D"card blue">
        <h3>Personalization</h3>
        <p>The agent knows the user's preferences, role, and history withou=
t re-asking on every session.</p>
      </div>
      <div class=3D"card green">
        <h3>Historical recall</h3>
        <p>Past events, prior decisions, and completed tasks are accessible=
 across sessions and agents.</p>
      </div>
      <div class=3D"card amber">
        <h3>Knowledge retention</h3>
        <p>Domain facts, SOPs, and learned workflows persist and can be reu=
sed without re-derivation.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <hr class=3D"divider">

    <h3>The three long-term memory types</h3>
    <p>The distinction is <strong>conceptual, not storage-based</strong>. A=
ll three may be stored in the same database. What differs is the type of in=
formation they represent.</p>

